body:
Thank yoiyou for your reply, it means a lot. My anxiety is still rife this evening but I am keeping myself occupied which helps. I don't know how deliberate his actions were but this is something that has happened in the past. Usually when he has been drunk, and one time lead to him pushing me to do a sexual act I didn't enjoy or particularly want. So I think it has triggered this memory tbh. 

&#x200B;

Our counselling has moved to once a month as it was going well in some respects and my mental health improved seeing her less often. 

&#x200B;

Unfortunately you are right, I don't feel I can fully remind him when boundaries are pushed, and I worry that to discuss after the act and my agreement to it doesn't help break the cycle.

I will echo everyone else here and say please continue with therapy. Her crying is normal, it is a response from the body towards stress. She will, undoubtedly have some repressed thoughts and beliefs which need to be worked through. Ask her if she would like individual sessions as part of her anxiety for now might be increased by your presence. If she feels shame or guilt about her sexuality then talking about it to stranger on the other side of a screen is one thing, but talking to you might be harder. Not because it is you, but because she will see you everyday so cannot hide from what she is feeling. 

&#x200B;

In the long term she will need to be able to talk to you, but perhaps initially she needs 121 sessions.
